{"id":"5029dc22071146399e59202633b2172c","model":"gpt-oss-120b","choices":[{"index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"{\n  \"answer\": \"noanswer\",\n  \"citations\": []\n}","reasoning_content":"We need to answer: \"What United Kingdom Prime Minister was succeeded in the 1990s by the Leader of the Conservative Party?\" The phrasing is ambiguous. Likely asking: Which UK Prime Minister was succeeded (i.e., replaced) in the 1990s by the Leader of the Conservative Party. In the 1990s, the Conservative Party leader became Prime Minister after succeeding a previous PM. In 1990, Margaret Thatcher was succeeded by John Major (who was Conservative Party leader). So the Prime Minister succeeded in the 1990s by the Leader of the Conservative Party: Margaret Thatcher was succeeded by John Major (who was the Conservative Party leader). However the question: \"What United Kingdom Prime Minister was succeeded in the 1990s by the Leader of the Conservative Party?\" Could also refer to 1997 when John Major (Conservative Party leader) was succeeded by Tony Blair (Labour). But the phrase \"by the Leader of the Conservative Party\" suggests the successor was the Leader of the Conservative Party. In 1990, John Major was the Leader of the Conservative Party and succeeded Margaret Thatcher as PM. So answer: Margaret Thatcher.\n\nWe need evidence. The provided evidence includes: \"These were the first annual local elections for the new Conservative Prime Minister Margaret Thatcher.\" That's about 1980. Not about succession. There's also evidence about Federation of Conservative Students referencing Margaret Thatcher. Not about succession. There's evidence about Conservative Party leadership election 1997 triggered when John Major resigned after defeat. That indicates John Major was leader and PM before 1997. But we need evidence that Margaret Thatcher was succeeded by John Major in 1990. Not present in given evidence. Let's search within evidence: There's no direct statement about Thatcher being succeeded. However we have \"The Federation of Conservative Students ... in reference to then Prime Minister Margaret Thatcher\". Not about succession.\n\nWe might need to infer from evidence: \"The 1997 Conservative Party leadership election was triggered ... John Major resigned ... following his party's defeat at the 1997 general election\". That indicates John Major was PM before 1997. But we need who he succeeded. Not given.\n\nMaybe the question expects answer \"Margaret Thatcher\". But we need citation. We don't have direct citation. Could we use evidence that John Major was Conservative leader and Prime Minister? Not present. There's no mention of John Major being PM. The evidence about Conservative Party (UK) includes \"The party leader, Theresa May, has served as both Leader of the Conservative Party and Prime Minister since 13 July 2016.\" Not relevant.\n\nThus we lack direct evidence for the succession. Could answer \"noanswer\". But maybe we can answer \"Margaret Thatcher\" with citation from evidence referencing her as Prime Minister in 1980, but not about succession. The question: \"What United Kingdom Prime Minister was succeeded in the 1990s by the Leader of the Conservative Party?\" The evidence includes \"These were the first annual local elections for the new Conservative Prime Minister Margaret Thatcher.\" That shows she was PM in 1980, but not about being succeeded. Not enough.\n\nWe could use evidence about John Major resignation in 1997, but that is about him being succeeded by Labour, not about who he succeeded. No.\n\nThus we cannot verify answer with evidence. So answer \"noanswer\"."},"finish_reason":"stop"}],"usage":{"prompt_tokens":1716,"completion_tokens":707,"total_tokens":2423,"audio_prompt_tokens":0},"created":1791982359,"object":"chat.completion","prompt_filter_results":null}